'' A DEFINED EXTERN LINKS ACROSS MODULES. One module writes 'extern e' then 'dim e = 5'
'' and the other only 'extern e': that is one object, and the link is the whole
'' assertion. The definition used to be emitted under the ordinary module spelling 'e'
'' while every reference went out as 'E$', so this stopped at
''
''     lld-link: error: undefined symbol: E$
''     >>> referenced by ...:(SHOWUSE)
''
'' -- verified by building the previous driver and running this against it. fbc links
'' and runs the same three sources, printing 'def 5' and 'use 5'.
''
'' THE EXIT CODE AND THE FILE ARE THE TEST. The harness does not run what it builds, so
'' what is asserted is that the linker resolved the symbol; tests/mir/extern-definition-name
'' pins the spelling itself, and the two programs' output was compared byte for byte
'' against fbc by hand.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -target windows-x86_64 tests/driver/src/externdef-main.bas tests/driver/src/externdef-def.bas tests/driver/src/externdef-use.bas -o tests/driver/externdef.exe
'' ENV: TKO_LIBPATH=toolchains/tiko-windows/lib/windows-x86_64
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-FILE: tests/driver/externdef.exe
'' EXPECT-NOT: undefined symbol
